{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon DynamoDB now supports percentile statistics for request latency - AWS","context":"Amazon DynamoDB has introduced support for percentile statistics for the SuccessfulRequestLatency CloudWatch metric. This new feature allows users to understand the distribution of latency for successful requests to DynamoDB, in addition to the existing average, minimum, and maximum statistics. The SuccessfulRequestLatency metric measures only the latency that is internal to the DynamoDB service and does not include client-side activity or network trip times. Users are advised to consider their end-to-end latency, which includes client-side activity, when analyzing latency. To factor in client-side activity, users can enable latency metric logging in their AWS SDK. The percentile statistic is available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. For more information on the percentile statistic and troubleshooting latency on DynamoDB, refer to the DynamoDB Metrics and Dimensions and Troubleshooting latency issues sections in the DynamoDB Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/03/amazon-dynamodb-percentile-statistics-request-latency/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon DynamoDB has introduced more granular throttle error exceptions with corresponding Amazon CloudWatch metrics. These new exceptions provide specific information about the resources causing throttling events and the reasons behind them. The new metrics and exceptions are available in all commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and China Regions. The throttling exceptions now include a list of reasons for the throttling and the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the affected table or index. These new reasons help diagnose and resolve throttling issues by allowing users to adjust throughput, switch to on-demand capacity mode, or optimize data access patterns. Users can refer to the \"Diagnosing throttling issues\" section in the DynamoDB developer guide, the \"Enhanced throttling observability in Amazon DynamoDB\" blog post, and the \"Troubleshooting throttling\" section in the developer guide for more information","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/amazon-dynamodb-more-granular-throttle-error-exceptions/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon DynamoDB, a managed NoSQL database service by AWS, has introduced a new feature that allows users to selectively emit events for throttled keys to CloudWatch Contributor Insights. This feature enables monitoring of throttled keys without emitting events for all accessed keys, thereby reducing costs. With this new mode, users can understand their traffic patterns and diagnose throttling-related issues by accessing information about their most accessed and throttled keys in a table or global secondary index. This feature is available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. To get started, users can refer to the CloudWatch Contributor Insights for DynamoDB section in the DynamoDB developer guide, the Enhanced throttling observability in Amazon DynamoDB blog post, and the Troubleshooting throttling section in the DynamoDB developer guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/amazon-dynamodb-cloudwatch-contributor-insights-throttled-keys/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-elasticache","skill_description":"Activate when developers have latent caching needs: slow API responses, database read bottlenecks, DynamoDB throttling or cost, RDS/Aurora scaling pressure, Bedrock latency or cost, or adding a cache; activate when working with Redis, Valkey, Memcached, or any in-memory data store, cache-aside patterns, session stores, rate limiting, leaderboards, counters, streams, queues, pub/sub, distributed locks, feature flags, shopping carts, or other caching strategies. Activate for GenAI and ML retrieval: vector similarity search for low-latency retrieval, semantic caching, RAG, LLM response caching, embedding stores, AI agent memory, recommendation, personalization. Activate for ElastiCache lifecycle: provisioning (serverless or node-based), engine selection, CloudFormation/CDK/Terraform IaC, VPC connectivity, TLS, RBAC, IAM auth, Global Datastore, monitoring, troubleshooting, cost optimization, and migration from self-managed Redis. Do not trigger for browser caches, CDN/CloudFront, HTTP Cache-Control, CPU caches.","skill_name":"amazon-elasticache"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"redshift-guide","skill_description":"Amazon Redshift is NOT PostgreSQL; corrects LLM mistakes; covers SQL, metadata, connectivity/auth, storage, query performance, and operations. Applies ONLY to Redshift (provisioned or Serverless). Pushes back on: CREATE INDEX, string_agg, pg_catalog, text type, SERIAL, stl_query. Triggers on: Redshift SQL, Redshift CREATE TABLE, Redshift COPY/UNLOAD, slow or suddenly slow Redshift query, EXPLAIN plan, queue wait, stale statistics/ANALYZE, result cache, Redshift permission denied, Redshift disk full, storage growth, snapshot/backup cost, ghost rows/bloat, VACUUM, spill to disk, distribution skew, Redshift system views, QUALIFY, PIVOT, MERGE, materialized views, Redshift Data API (Python/boto3), Redshift workload management (WLM), concurrency scaling, Redshift resize, Spectrum, awsdatacatalog, connection failures (SSL/TLS, ODBC/JDBC, connection limit), IAM/SAML federation, IAM Identity Center, datashares. Does NOT apply to: S3 policies, Athena, Glue/Iceberg outside Redshift, Aurora, RDS, DynamoDB.\n\nServices: redshift, redshift-serverless, redshift-data-api\nTasks: query, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, database-administrator, data-analyst\nWorkload: data-warehouse, analytics, etl","skill_name":"redshift-guide"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify how DynamoDB SuccessfulRequestLatency relates to throttled request metrics."}
